cult  for that freeze dryer to maintain a  temperature but it's also going to  create melt back of your sample during  the winter you don't want the unit under  a heat vent because again you're you're  blowing keep down onto a frozen sample  and that could have issues with melt  back of your samples itself if you are  doing the pre free step in a separate  freezer you don't want that freezer  three buildings down where you have to  walk two miles to get it to the freeze  dryer because obviously by the time it  gets to the freeze dryer it could be  melted so a couple things to keep in  mind and I have seen examples of all of  these so when I talk about these  examples if they're not they're not made  up there are real-life examples your  final outcome sometimes you get a very  pretty  powder a pretty cake or a pretty outcome  but sometimes you don't every once in a  while you'll have you'll need to add  bulking agents or stabilizers if your  sample is prone to being degraded by  freezing you may have to use a leiyo  protectant to help against that pre  freezing step  next we're going to talk a little bit  about the C GMP requirements and  according to the FDA that if you want a  cgmp piece of equipment that's actually  done on your end so you can take the  piece of equipment and make it fit in  need of your GMP requirements what a  manufacturer can do for you is we can  offer you the materials of construction  we can give you the test reports that we  have done on your freeze dryer based on  your serial number to let you know that  it did pass all of our internal QA  specifications we also offer all the  manuals the documents the videos  everything that you need we do i qo q we  offer it at no charge  PQ would be done by an outside  corporation that you would contact to  come in to finish up your qualification  but we are always happy to help in any  of the steps and whatever you need to  make sure that your freeze dryer can be  used with your cgmp standards  there are multiple challenging samples  and as freeze-drying  evolved into a sample prep step versus a  long-term storage step we're seeing more  and more challenging samples one of them  that I have seen is called tert-butyl  alcohol so on paper tert-butyl looks  like it should freeze dry fine it has a  great eutectic temperature there should  be no issues however when you actually  freeze dry it you don't get a nice  powder you actually get a gummy type  sample so very difficult to reconstitute  you may have issues keeping it keeping  it dry because you've already kind of  got that that gummy wet that you can't  get totally dry so as a sample prep step  the tert-butyl is fine as long as you're  going to use it right away but obviously  for long-term storage it's already  starting to rehydrate so you're going to  have issues with that long-term another  product that is very difficult to dry  our alcohols alcohol such as ethanol and  methanol have a very low freezing price  freezing temperature and as Megan noted  you must have a temperature differential  between your collector and your samples  freezing point of about 20 degrees the  for example ethanol has a freezing point  of minus 117 degrees which would mean  that you would need a freeze dryer of  minus one hundred and thirty-seven  degrees there's not a freezer out there  that can go that low so how do you get  around if you need to freeze dry  lyophilization I'm sorry freeze dry  ethanol samples what you'll do is you  must use a minus 105 freeze dryer and  you must dilute those samples to ten  percent or less 10 percent is the spot  that we have found in our testing where  that sample does not melt back but it  does go through the freeze dry process  so 10 percent is your magic number  whenever you're doing alcohol based  samples and you have to have a minus 105  degree freeze dryer  DMSO is another solvent that we're  seeing more and more often  DMSO is difficult because that solvent  just the solvent alone is going to start  attacking rubber and plastic components  and anytime you have a anytime you have  anything under vacuum such as a lieth  eliezer it's got to use rubber  components for the gaskets and the  o-rings to keep that under a deep vacuum  so what we recommend with DMSO is it  will freeze dry fine just watch all of  your rubber and plastic components and  replace as needed we've also found that  sugars and salts cause a lower eutectic  temperature so we do recommend a cascade  model we also have seen with samples  such as fruit that the sugars bypassed  the collector and they could get into  your pump so a hepa filter in line with  the pump is recommended again with soil  samples and we do recommend a HEPA  filter with those as well because what  happens with soil samples is during the  dry process they could become very  powdery and they're going to bypass a  collector and go right into the vacuum  pump so be very careful with soil  samples they're great they freeze very  very nicely same with Leafs tissues they  freeze dry nicely just watch the  composition of those samples if you need  a HEPA filter to protect the pump that's  something that can be easily added in  line if you have whole fruit samples or  whole animal samples that just fish it's  best to either homogenize the fruits and  vegetables or if you can poke holes in  the tissues of your animals or poke  holes in the fruit if your freeze drying  for example grapes or blueberries poking  holes through those skins will be  helpful  a big part of freeze-drying is going to  be troubleshooting if your samples won't  stay frozen during the freeze dry  process there's a couple things that you  can look at make sure that you have the  correct freeze dryer as Megan mentioned  you must have a temperature differential  between the freeze dryer and the samples  eutectic temperature for example with  the Seton nitrile which has a eutectic  temperature of minus 42 you must have a  freeze dryer that is minus 62 degrees or  colder in this case we would recommend a  cascade model which reaches minus 84  degrees if your samples are small or in  plastic containers  those tend to melt a lot quicker even  walking from your freezer to your freeze  dryer and if your samples have alcohols  in them alcohols have a very low  freezing point which require a minus 105  degree freeze dryer as well as diluting  those alcohols to 10 percent or less  also take a look at the pressure of your  freeze dryer because if your pump is not  pulling correct pressure that could also  lead to melting back of your samples  sometimes a sample can be very  lightweight or very powdery and they'll  get pulled out by the vacuum so using  filter paper or parafilm is a way around  that it you'll still notice that your  samples might pop up in the tube but if  you can put some pair of them over at  least you won't lose your sample if your  samples are taking a long time to lie  applies your shelf temperature might be  too cold your sample may be too thick  or you might be somehow blocking a vial  if you have a vial inside of a flask you  may not have enough headspace to  encourage those molecules to leave your  vial  a couple things to check if you're  noticing that you're having a vacuum  level that's not what it used to be make  sure none of your gaskets have cracks in  them or any dirt on them sometimes we'll  see dust or dirt on the gaskets and even  little specks of it can prevent pulling  a deep vacuum so check your gaskets  check all of your valves and always do  an oil change if you're unsure when the  last oil change was it's probably time  to do another oil change oil changes  really are dependent on how often you  use the freeze dryer as well as what  solvents you're using in your free  Schreyer because if solvents are  bypassing and getting into your pump you  will have issues we also recommend doing  a purge on your pump before and after  running your freeze dryer if your  samples are not sealing corr